= 1I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= i
{parent=1i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Lagrange theorem for polynomial congruences> says that a nonzero <polynomial> of degree $d$ over a <field> has at most $d$ distinct <roots of a polynomial>. It follows by repeatedly factoring out $x-a$ at a root. Over the <finite field> $\mathbb F_p$, the only solutions of $x^2=1$ are $1$ and $-1$: these are distinct because $p$ is odd, and the root bound excludes any others.

Every nonzero element has a unique <multiplicative inverse>. Pair each element of $\mathbb F_p^\times$ with its inverse. All pairs of distinct elements contribute $1$ to the product; the only unpaired elements are $1$ and $-1$. Thus <Wilson theorem> follows:
$$
\boxed{(p-1)!\equiv-1\pmod p.}
$$
If Lagrange's theorem is formulated for <finite groups>, it states that the order of a <subgroup> divides the order of the group; in particular every element of $\mathbb F_p^\times$ has order dividing $p-1$. The inverse-pairing proof above establishes the required congruence directly.

= ii
{parent=1i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $m=(p-1)/2$ and let $O=1\cdot3\cdot5\cdots(p-2)$. The even residues $2,4,\ldots,p-1$ are precisely the negatives, in reverse order, of the odd residues $1,3,\ldots,p-2$. Hence their product is $(-1)^mO$ in the <finite field> $\mathbb F_p$. Applying <Wilson theorem> gives
$$
-1\equiv(p-1)!=O\,((-1)^mO)=(-1)^mO^2\pmod p.
$$
The initial factor $1^2$ can be omitted, so
$$
\boxed{3^2\cdot5^2\cdots(p-2)^2\equiv(-1)^{m+1}=(-1)^{(p+1)/2}\pmod p.}
$$
For $p=3$ the product is empty and equals $1$, consistently with the formula.

= 2F
{parent=Paper 3}
